---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../components/CaseStudy.astro';
import ArcMotif from '../components/ArcMotif.astro';
---
<Layout title="Art & Architecture" description="Architecture and design projects — AIA New Hampshire, Scholastic Art & Writing, Cornell Summer Architecture." slug="design-work">
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <span class="eyebrow">Art &amp; Architecture</span>
    <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,2.8rem); margin-top:0.5rem;">The Studio</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Three projects, each pushing a little further from the drafting table into how people
      actually move through and feel a space. This is the work I want you to see first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <CaseStudy
      num="01 — AIA New Hampshire, 1st Place"
      title="Arc Resonance Center"
      tags={["Community Architecture", "AIA New Hampshire", "1st Place · $650"]}
      images={[
        { src: 'images/aia/aia-03.jpg', alt: 'Ari Lee with the student design team in front of the Arc Resonance Center presentation boards at the AIA New Hampshire competition' },
        { src: 'images/aia/aia-02.jpg', alt: 'Close-up of the Arc Resonance Center presentation boards, showing the site plan, floor plans, and elevations' },
        { src: 'images/aia/aia-05.jpg', alt: 'Close-up of the Arc Resonance Center axonometric renderings and material palette' },
        { src: 'images/aia/aia-01.jpg', alt: 'Ari Lee holding the AIA New Hampshire 1st place certificate for Arc Resonance Center, with Heather Taylor of AIA NH' },
        { src: 'images/aia/aia-06.jpg', alt: 'Ari Lee at the awards ceremony holding the "Project of Distinction" certificate for Arc Resonance Center' },
        { src: 'images/aia/aia-04.jpg', alt: 'The AIA New Hampshire awards reception, with the Arc Resonance Center boards on display behind the audience' },
      ]}
    >
      <p>
        Arc Resonance Center is a community music building that reads as two semicircles
        radiating outward like sound waves, housing a performance hall and classrooms. The form
        is the argument: a building about music should look like it's making sound, not just
        containing it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Won 1st place in the statewide AIA New Hampshire High School Design Challenge.
      </p>
    </CaseStudy>

    <CaseStudy
      num="02 — Scholastic Art & Writing Awards, Silver Key"
      title="A Playground for Ithaca State Park"
      tags={["Landscape / Play", "Scholastic Art & Writing", "Silver Key"]}
      images={[
        { src: 'images/ithaca-playground/ithaca-playground-02.jpg', alt: 'Rendering of the Ithaca State Park playground at sunset, showing the diving platform, bridge, and ladder structure over the water' },
        { src: 'images/ithaca-playground/ithaca-playground-01.jpg', alt: 'Hand-colored concept diagram for the playground, annotated with Walk, Swim, Dive, Climb, Run, Picnic, and Rest' },
        { src: 'images/ithaca-playground/ithaca-playground-03.jpg', alt: 'Four elevation and section studies of the climbing structure, diving platform, and seating' },
        { src: 'images/ithaca-playground/ithaca-playground-04.jpg', alt: 'Physical model of the playground, built from wood and cardboard, showing the mast, ladder ramp, and stepping stones' },
        { src: 'images/ithaca-playground/ithaca-playground-05.jpg', alt: 'Detail of the physical model showing the platform railings and stepped seating' },
        { src: 'images/ithaca-playground/ithaca-playground-06.jpg', alt: 'Detail of the physical model showing the ladder, mast, and stone paths' },
      ]}
    >
      <p>
        Designed for Stewart Park around the concept of attraction and framing: instead of
        inventing focal points from a blank site, every structure — the climbing bars, the
        viewing platform, the diving tower — is centered on something already there. Bold color
        is used deliberately to guide movement and draw people through the site, not as
        decoration applied after the plan was finished.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recognized with a Silver Key in the national Scholastic Art &amp; Writing Awards.
      </p>
    </CaseStudy>

    <CaseStudy
      num="03 — Cornell University Summer Architecture Program"
      title="Studio Semester at Cornell"
      tags={["Rhino 3D", "Technical Drawing", "Model Making", "6 Weeks · 60 hrs/week"]}
      images={[
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-01.jpg', alt: 'Ari Lee and program cohort holding sketchbooks in front of Cornell\'s McGraw Tower and Uris Library' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-06.jpg', alt: 'Precedent research wall on Luis Barragán\'s Casa Gilardi, with a "Levitating / Framed / Attracting" diagram and a folded-paper study' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-07.jpg', alt: 'Detailed pencil study of radiating structural lines converging at two points' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-08.jpg', alt: 'An early radiating stick-and-block structural study model' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-10.jpg', alt: 'A woven wood-stick lattice cube, a structural material study' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-09.jpg', alt: 'An abstract black paper-cut model exploring a bridge-like structure between two towers' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-04.jpg', alt: 'Desk setup with a laptop open to a rendering, a sketchbook of light and shadow studies, and drafting tools' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-05.jpg', alt: 'An in-progress cardboard model on a desk, with the "A Building in Stewart Park" project brief pinned behind it' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-11.jpg', alt: 'The final studio model: a wood and cardboard pavilion with a mast, ladder ramp, and stepping stones' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-12.jpg', alt: 'The final model photographed on-site, overlooking a real gorge and waterfall' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-13.jpg', alt: 'Detail of the final model with a live ivy vine woven through the mast structure' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-14.jpg', alt: 'Detail of the final model\'s skate ramp and picnic area, with a miniature skateboard and picnic props' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-02.jpg', alt: 'Overhead view of the studio crit space, walls lined with drawings and tables covered in architectural models' },
        { src: 'images/cornell-summer/cornell-summer-03.jpg', alt: 'Ari Lee with three program friends in front of a wall of studio renderings' },
      ]}
    >
      <p>
        An intensive six-week program covering Rhino 3D modeling, Adobe Illustrator, Photoshop,
        Lightroom, architectural photography, and technical drawing — producing a full studio
        portfolio of renderings, physical models, technical drawings, and site plans.
      </p>
      <p>
        Research component focused on Luis Barragán's Casa Gilardi and Louis Kahn's Class of 1945
        Library — a building I'd photograph again later, for an entirely different project (see
        Film &amp; Photography).
      </p>
    </CaseStudy>
  </section>

  <div class="arc-divider"><ArcMotif size={48} /></div>

  <section class="section section--tight">
    <span class="eyebrow">Also on the boards</span>
    <ul class="awards-list" style="margin-top:0.8rem;">
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">Young Architecture and Design Contest</span><span class="award-detail">National competition — submitted September 2026</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">2026</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">Architecture Foundation of San Francisco</span><span class="award-detail">3rd place</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">2026</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><span class="award-title">Exeter Architecture Club</span><span class="award-detail">Meeting practicing architects, visiting active construction sites, sketching and modeling buildings around campus</span></div>
        <span class="award-year">2023–Present</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="caption" style="margin-top:1.2rem;">More artwork and renderings coming soon.</p>
  </section>
</Layout>
